            <div xml:id="d113">

                <pb facs="0212" n="142" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv01/pg142"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1140/255: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Special Representative</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >House</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-27T11:00:00+00:00"
                            >November 27, 1918—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 27—9:53 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>184. Secret for the President. I have talked the matter over with Admiral Benson and I suggest that you take the
                    southern route and land at Marseille. At this time of year the chances are very
                    much in favor of your having far milder and more pleasant weather on the
                    southern route than on the northern one. That was our experience last year.
                        Benson tells me that it will only
                    take 2 days longer and that if you sail on the 4th you would arrive at Marseille
                    on the 14th. This would bring you to Paris in plenty of time for the Conference.
                    Will not you please let me know what you decide [to] do.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward House</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
